A recurring failure mode is guessing a motif's footprint or anchor from an incomplete visual impression. Before submitting, extract the exact colored motif from the input, list its non-background cells as offsets from a chosen anchor, and infer how the anchor corresponds to each scattered marker. Then apply the proposed rule to all markers in the demo and reproduce every output cell, including preserved cells and clipping at boundaries. Use demos to resolve orientation/anchor ambiguity rather than repeatedly submitting speculative grids. Distinct instances may share an abstract operation while using different motifs, so transfer the operation but relearn each motif's geometry and anchor from its own examples.

Also audit output dimensions and row lengths mechanically when possible; a syntactically plausible grid may fail the ARC grid schema if any row differs in length.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/case-study-cross-motif-inference.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/cross-demo-role-abstraction.md", "content": "# Reconciling diverse demonstrations

Continual-ARC demos can vary radically in grid size, palette, and apparent motif. Do not conclude they are unrelated solely from those differences, and do not unify them based on superficial shared colors or density. For each pair, extract the exact input-output changes and describe the transformation in role-level terms (background, marker/seed, copied footprint, output color). Compare those abstractions across all pairs. A compelling candidate must reproduce every demo cell-for-cell, including cells left unchanged and explicit background-valued positions in any stamped footprint. If no common validated rule emerges, another demo is more valuable than a speculative submission when the remaining budget permits.

A useful audit table records per demo: dimensions; color roles; seed/motif offsets; changed cells; proposed abstract operation; exact replay result. Distinguish a genuinely conditional rule from an incorrect attempt to force one unconditional template. Keep the final action protocol exact regardless of analysis length.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/cross-demo-role-abstraction.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/mixed-demo-consistency.md", "content": "# Mixed and identity demonstrations

A task's demos are evidence to reconcile, not examples to cherry-pick. Before inferring a rule, compare each pair's dimensions, palette, geometry, and exact input/output difference. If a new pair has different dimensions/palette or is an identity mapping while earlier pairs showed changes, do not force a single transformation from superficial commonalities. Consider conditional task variants or an unresolved inconsistency; request a further demo only if it can distinguish hypotheses. Validate every proposed rule against every pair before submission. An incorrect submission should trigger re-analysis of the rule and the full evidence set rather than another unvalidated guess.
